## What happened

In **.NET 11**, **DSA was removed from macOS**. Calling `DSA.Create()` (and any DSA signing/verification) now throws a `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS. It still works on Windows and Linux.

## How to fix it

Since you need to **verify legacy DSA signatures**, you have two options:

### Option A — Platform-guard DSA (keep it for Win/Linux)

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException("DSA is not supported on macOS in .NET 11+");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

### Option B — Migrate to ECDSA or RSA (recommended long-term)

DSA is a legacy algorithm regardless of platform. If you control the signing side, re-sign with a modern algorithm:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
bool valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

If you **must** verify existing DSA signatures on macOS, you'll need to use a third-party crypto library (e.g., BouncyCastle) for the macOS path, since the built-in implementation is gone.
